                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/7412: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Japan</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Neville</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-11-21T12:00:00+09:00">November 21,
                        1935—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received 1:05 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>213. Department’s 178, November 20, 4 p.m. So far as the Embassy has been able to
                    learn no such statement has been issued by the Foreign Office nor have there
                    been official pronouncements of a like nature. It has been the practice of the
                    Foreign Office spokesman to respect foreign correspondents and answer or parry
                    their questions. Yesterday he seems to have been in a communicative mood and
                    told the assembled newspapermen on his own responsibility that the new regime in
                    North China would probably resemble the Canton government in being a sort of
                    autonomous government but that of course the whole matter was a domestic Chinese
                    question. The fact of Japanese “assistance” was not denied.</p>
                <p>Differing versions of this interview have appeared in the local press and were
                    cabled to the United States. None of the correspondents recall having cabled the
                    wording quoted by the Department but it is possible that the expression crept in
                    during the course of distribution.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Neville</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
